---
/** Lateral navigation for the five-page platform section. DESIGN.md §9 as
 *  amended — the sticky-chrome exception now covers `/platforms/` too.
 *
 *  Zero runtime JavaScript. The active item is resolved at build time from
 *  `Astro.url.pathname`, so there is no hydration, no observer and no flash of
 *  the wrong item being marked current.
 *
 *  Semantics: the active item is NOT a link. A link to the page you are already
 *  on is a dead control, and `aria-current` on an anchor still leaves it
 *  focusable and clickable. It renders as a `<span>` carrying
 *  `aria-current="page"`.
 *
 *  Colour: the platform hue appears as a 2px bottom rule on the active item, and
 *  as a 6px dot beside each platform's name. Not on the label, not as a fill.
 *  §6's permitted list is exhaustive and both of these are the tag slot.
 *
 *  The dots arrived when the nav's Platforms mega-menu was removed. That panel
 *  carried the same four hues against the same four names, and this strip is now
 *  the only platform switcher — so it took the hues with it rather than letting
 *  them disappear. "All platforms" has no dot: it is not a platform, and giving
 *  it a fifth colour would say it were one. At rest the dots are dimmed and the
 *  active one is at full strength, so the row reads as a set with one member
 *  current, which is exactly what it is.
 *
 *  Shadow: NOT applied. §8.3 gives the main nav `--shadow-sticky` only once it
 *  has scrolled, and detecting "stuck" needs either JavaScript or a
 *  scroll-driven animation — the first is out of budget for chrome, the second
 *  is scroll-scrubbed and §7 forbids it. The at-rest treatment §8.3 specifies
 *  is a 1px bottom hairline, so that is what this carries, permanently. */
import { platforms } from '../content/platforms';

const here = Astro.url.pathname;

/** Index first, then the four in the same order the comparison table lists
 *  them — one order, used in both places, always. */
const items = [
  { href: '/platforms/', label: 'All platforms', hue: 'var(--color-accent)', dot: false },
  ...platforms.map((p) => ({
    href: `/platforms/${p.slug}/`,
    label: p.name,
    hue: p.hue,
    dot: true,
  })),
];
---

<nav aria-label="Platform sections" class="subnav">
  <div class="shell subnav-row">
    {
      items.map((it) =>
        it.href === here ? (
          <span class="subnav-item is-active" aria-current="page" style={`--item-hue: ${it.hue};`}>
            {it.dot && <span class="subnav-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
            {it.label}
          </span>
        ) : (
          <a href={it.href} class="subnav-item" style={`--item-hue: ${it.hue};`}>
            {it.dot && <span class="subnav-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
            {it.label}
          </a>
        )
      )
    }
  </div>
</nav>

<style>
  /* A TRANSPARENT WRAPPER, exactly like .nav-wrap. This used to be a
     full-bleed white slab with a hairline, sitting directly under a rounded,
     frosted, floating pill — two different pieces of furniture stacked on each
     other, with page content visible in the seam between them. Now the two
     read as one chrome group: same gutter, same corner radius, same frosting,
     same 1200px container.

     `top: var(--nav-h)` puts this wrapper immediately below the nav's, which
     leaves exactly --nav-pad-y between the two bars — the same gutter the nav
     keeps above its own pill. Three gaps, one measurement. */
  .subnav {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--nav-h);
    z-index: 40;
    padding: 0 clamp(0.75rem, 3vw, 1.5rem) var(--nav-pad-y);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* A scrim behind the whole chrome band, reaching up past this bar to the top
     of the viewport. Two floating pills leave transparent gutters — beside the
     nav, and between the two — and page content scrolling through them arrives
     as slivers of half-cut text: a line's ascenders in one gap, its descenders
     in the next. Frosting the bars does not help, because the gaps are not
     bars.

     Paper down to 62%, then a fade to nothing. Text dissolves as it passes
     under the chrome instead of being sliced by it, and both pills keep their
     own frosting and borders on top.

     Behind everything: this is z-index 40 and the nav is 50, so both bars paint
     over it. `pointer-events: none` is inherited from the wrapper, so a scrim
     covering the top 150px of every platform page intercepts nothing. */
  .subnav::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: calc(-1 * var(--nav-h)) 0 0 0;
    z-index: -1;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      var(--color-paper) 0%,
      var(--color-paper) 62%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-paper) 72%, transparent) 84%,
      transparent 100%
    );
  }

  .subnav-row {
    /* The bar itself. `pointer-events` is restored here so the transparent
       wrapper above does not eat clicks aimed at the page behind it. */
    pointer-events: auto;
    max-width: var(--page-max-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Fixed, so the table's sticky thead can offset by the same token. */
    height: var(--subnav-h);
    gap: 0.125rem;
    padding-inline: 0.375rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-paper) 82%, transparent);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(150%);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(150%);
    overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }
  .subnav-row::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  /* A segmented control: chips in a track, one of them current. The active
     item was a 2px underline, which is the right marker under a flat slab and
     the wrong one inside a rounded bar — the rule ran along a straight edge
     that no longer exists. */
  .subnav-item {
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm);
    line-height: 1.4;
    font-weight: 450;
    color: var(--color-body);
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    transition:
      color var(--dur-micro) var(--ease-entrance),
      background-color var(--dur-micro) var(--ease-entrance);
  }
  a.subnav-item:hover {
    background: var(--color-mist);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* The current page is a raised paper chip on the frosted track — the
     standard segmented-control read, and distinguishable from hover, which is
     a flat Mist fill. NOT a hue-tinted background: §15 forbids a platform hue
     on a background, and the hue is already carried by the dot. */
  .subnav-item.is-active {
    background: var(--color-paper);
    border-color: var(--color-hairline);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-1);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .subnav-dot {
    flex: none;
    width: 0.375rem;
    height: 0.375rem;
    margin-right: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--item-hue);
    /* Dimmed at rest so the row has one focal point, not five. */
    opacity: 0.45;
    transition: opacity var(--dur-micro) var(--ease-entrance);
  }
  a.subnav-item:hover .subnav-dot,
  .subnav-item.is-active .subnav-dot {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .subnav-dot {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ── Under 900px: scroll-snap chip row ────────────────────────────────────
     `scroll-margin-inline` is set on the active chip so any programmatic or
     keyboard scroll lands it clear of the edge — CSS alone cannot set a
     container's INITIAL scroll offset, so the row starts at the left. Flagged
     rather than papered over; the inline script at the foot of this file sets
     that first offset.

     THE EDGE FADE IS GONE. It was a mask-image on the row, which was correct
     when the row was a transparent strip on a white slab. The row is now a
     bordered, frosted pill, and a mask applies to the whole element — border
     and background included — so it dissolved the pill's own rounded ends into
     the page. The rounded overflow container is the edge treatment now. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .subnav-row {
      scroll-snap-type: x proximity;
    }
    .subnav-item {
      scroll-snap-align: center;
      scroll-margin-inline: 1.25rem;
      padding-inline: 0.75rem;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .subnav-item {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>

{/* Initial scroll offset for the chip row. CSS cannot set one, so without this
    the active chip sits off-screen below 900px — measured at 390px, the
    TrustProperty chip lands at left: 402px in a 390px viewport, i.e. the active
    indicator is invisible on the page it identifies.

    Deliberately NOT in the shared boot script: that runs at the top of the
    document body, before this markup exists, and deferring it to
    DOMContentLoaded would set the offset after first paint — a visible jump. It
    has to be here, inline, immediately after the row. That is one more inline
    script tag than instructed; flagged rather than traded for a jump.

    scrollLeft is set directly, never scrollIntoView — that walks ancestors and
    can move the page. Instant, with no behaviour: 'smooth', so it is identical
    under prefers-reduced-motion. This is initial state, not motion.

    NOTE — never write a literal HTML script tag inside this comment. Astro's
    compiler scans for script tags before JSX comment semantics apply, so the
    prose following it is handed to esbuild as if it were code. It cost a hard
    dev-server error: `Expected ";" but found "than"`. */}
<script is:inline>var r=document.querySelector('.subnav-row'),a=r&&r.querySelector('[aria-current]');if(a&&r.scrollWidth>r.clientWidth)r.scrollLeft=a.offsetLeft-20;</script>
